id=a52sxq_soundtrigger_no_hal_reboot
name=Sound trigger: no audio HAL restart during calls; the resync restart runs after the call
author=kusti420
description=Hardening for the call audio report of 2026-10-22 ("I hear them for a second, then neither side hears anything", CS calls and WhatsApp, clean flash of the latest build). On the A52s vendor the sound trigger HAL lives inside the audio HAL process (android.hardware.audio.service), so every SoundTriggerHalEnforcer.reboot() (setprop sys.audio.restart.hal 1, audioserver.rc stops/starts the audio HAL) kills the audio HAL: a running call loses its voice/VoIP session in both directions, and audioserver restarts. That is the only mechanism seen so far on this ROM that kills all call audio at once. It is proven in the 9.3.62 tester log (logsrecentes.txt: "Exception caught from HAL, rebooting HAL" -> media.audio_flinger died), and calls are exactly when the HAL is pushed onto its non-LPI path ("lpi NOT supported due to concurrency"). The reboot is also what resyncs the middleware after a failed HAL call: stopRecognition marks the model PENDING_STOP before the HAL call and INACTIVE only on success, unloadSoundModel drops the model only on success, and SoundTriggerModule / Validation / SoundTriggerHelper above update only on success too. The HAL death after the reboot makes SoundTriggerModule.binderDied detach the old enforcer, attach a fresh one (empty model table) and send onModuleDied to every client (Validation marks the session DEAD, SoundTriggerHelper clears its model state, re-attaches, clients reload). The previous version of this patch never rebooted on a HAL status, so such a model could stay dead until the phone rebooted (part6 review M1). Now (K4StRetry, src in soundtrigger_load_no_reboot): (1) SoundTriggerHalEnforcer.handleException, the branch for anything that is not a RecoverableException or a dead HAL, calls K4StRetry.halError. (2) The 6 ModelCallbackEnforcer contract checks (event for an unknown model, recognitionStillActive with a non-success status, unload event for an unknown or active model) keep their wtfStack log and call K4StRetry.callbackError instead of reboot(); the event is dropped as in stock. Both decide by the AudioManager mode (OnModeChangedListener, registered when the HAL stack attaches): MODE_NORMAL -> stock, log "Exception caught from HAL, rebooting HAL" and reboot now. Any other mode (ringing, IN_CALL, IN_COMMUNICATION/VoIP, CALL_SCREENING, redirect) or the mode is not known yet (early boot) -> no reboot; a HalException goes to the client as RecoverableException(5, INTERNAL_ERROR); the reboot is owed and runs 5 s after the mode is back to NORMAL (cancelled if a call starts again, skipped if that HAL stack died on its own meanwhile). Load refusals never reboot (soundtrigger_load_no_reboot). Still rebooted at once: SoundTriggerHalWatchdog timeouts (a hung HAL call; deferring would keep the middleware's binder callers blocked for the call). NOT proven to be the cause of the 2026-10-22 report. Logcat: grep K4StRetry ("not rebooting the HAL (shared with call audio)", "call ended: rebooting the sound trigger HAL") and SoundTriggerHalEnforcer.
